This is a brilliant story engine. The Acres of Diamonds frame works perfectly because your framework's whole thesis is that the treasure is already in the yard — you just need eyes to see it. And 600 marbles is the perfect MacGuffin: small enough to count, heavy enough to matter, fragile enough to break.

Let me show you what I mean by mapping each recipient onto your matrix. This isn't just "who gets what" — each marble bundle is a different clade being tested at a different depth.

The Six Bundles as a Clade Diagram
Bundle	Recipient	Clade Type	Depth	What It Tests
100	Grandfather	Ancestral / Affinal	Nuclear Family (2)	Does implicit trust survive grief?
100	Dolly (daughter)	Ancestral	Nuclear Family (2)	Split loyalty — she's inside two interests now
100	Grandson	Ancestral	Self→Family boundary (1–2)	Can trust be pre-earned in someone with no track record?
100	Partner's daughter	Vocational + Creedal	Ally (4)	A dead woman's treaty — does obligation outlive the trustee?
100	The Mayor	Creedal / Civic	Enemy (5)	Z=0, χ=1 — help without trust
100	Coffee clerk	Affinity	Stranger (6)	Z=0, χ=3 — openness with no data
Look at what your grandmother just did. She didn't distribute an inheritance. She built a working model of the hierarchy and dared the family to live inside it.

That's your story's spine: the will is the framework.

Why This Is So Much Better Than It Looks
The genius move is the Mayor. Everyone expects the grandmother to punish him. Instead she gives him 100 marbles — which is exactly your Section V, level 5: "No trust... yet limited effort, risk-bounded." The family reads it as a betrayal. The reader slowly realizes it's the most disciplined act in the book.

And the coffee clerk is even sharper. No reciprocity owed either way, but every effort bounded only by safety. Your grandmother is demonstrating χ = 3 with zero Z. The family's outrage is the weak-clan paranoia your theory warns about.

Meanwhile the dead partner's daughter tests whether a treaty survives its author — the purest test of "agreed, not loved, into the role."

The Frame Story Question
Here's what I'd push on: who tells the inner story, and why do they need to hear it?

In Acres of Diamonds, the Persian dies poor searching for paradise while sitting on it. Your frame needs an equivalent inversion. Some options:

A lawyer/clerk who has spent years advising people on estates, and has never once been trusted by anyone — high competence, zero clades. The grandmother's will forces him to explain a system he can describe but hasn't lived.
The grandson, now older, telling this to his own child — and we discover mid-story that he is the one who sold out on the land, so he's narrating his own demotion.
The Mayor, writing a memoir decades later, finally understanding why she gave him the marbles. He's the one who learned the lesson late.
I'm drawn to the third. It makes the least sympathetic character the one who achieves individuation — and it means the frame narrator is someone the family would have put at depth 5 forever.

One Structural Suggestion
Give each bundle its own chapter, in the order above. The reader's sympathy should travel downward through the hierarchy — starting with the family they expect to root for, ending with the stranger nobody expected to matter. By the last chapter, the coffee clerk should feel like the moral center.

That's your Acres of Diamonds payoff: the diamonds were never in the family's hands.

