Because now she knew. She had the information, updated the document anyway, and told him to his face. And then she ran out of time before she could finish the thought.

What This Changes
The grandmother is no longer a victim of stale paperwork. She made a deliberate choice with incomplete conviction — and died in the gap between decision and revision. That's devastatingly human, and it makes her a person rather than an oracle.

Ask what "on a whim" means here. A whim isn't random — it's a decision made faster than the reasons can be assembled. So what was the whim? Some possibilities worth weighing:

She left it to him to bind him. If he inherits, he can't raze her land. The bequest was a leash disguised as a gift. She believed a man who accepts your money won't take your property.
She left it to him as an argument. Not a bribe — a demonstration. This is what we think of you; act accordingly. Faith as leverage.
She left it to him because she couldn't bear to be the kind of person who cuts people off. Her own integration failed on exactly this question. Even dense players have one untraversed road.
I'm partial to the first. It makes her clever, wrong, and loving all at once — and it means her plan failed, because she misread which clade he was actually serving. She assumed Affinity would outrank Creed. He didn't. Your Split Loyalty section, dramatized from the inside.

Why Telling Him Makes It Worse (In the Best Way)
He now knows three things simultaneously:

She trusted him enough to say it aloud
He took the road anyway
He never told her to stop
And crucially — he accepted the bequest without refusing it or warning her. That's not passive. Under your framework, accepting trust while serving a competing interest, without declaring, is the exact definition of the undeclared split. He's not a villain. He's committed the single most dangerous move in the entire system.

So when the family learns he knew, their fury becomes legible rather than paranoid. They're not overreacting. Their instinct is correct even if their response will be excessive. That's much better writing than giving them a cartoon grievance.

The Scene That Now Writes Itself
Not the will reading. The last conversation.

You should decide whether the reader sees it early or late. Two options:

Early: We watch her tell him. Warm, funny, two people who like each other. Then he goes home and signs the eminent domain notice. The rest of the book is the aftermath, and the reader carries the knowledge like a stone. Brutal, but controlled.
Late: The family discovers mid-book that he knew. Everything they've been feeling gets retroactive justification — and the reader has to confront how much they were rooting for him. Sharper twist, but riskier.
I'd choose early. Your framework is about pattern recognition over incident, so letting the reader see the pattern form beats ambushing them with a reveal.

One Detail I'd Plant
She told him with no obligation, you said. Have her say something like: "Don't thank me. And don't you dare make it a reason."

Then the whole tragedy lives inside that sentence. She saw it coming. She named the failure mode. She did it anyway.

