Here is the timeline of the betrayal, and why it breaks the family’s trust so thoroughly:

Maud tells the Mayor: She builds rapport. She signals intent. This creates an Affinity Clade bond. He feels seen, supported, validated in his civic mission. He thinks, Maud is on my side.
Maud changes the will: She makes it legal. The paper now says Ally.
Mayor starts planning the road: He is acting on his Creedal Clade duty. He likely believes he can have both: the road and Maud’s goodwill. He doesn’t think of it as a betrayal because he thinks the will proves she supports him. He’s operating on old data (her support) to justify new actions (the road).
Maud dies: The window for correction closes. She never gets to say, “Hey, I heard you’re breaking up my yard.” He never gets to say, “I’m still doing it, but I’m sorry.” She dies with a will that says he’s family, while his actions say he’s an enemy.
The Dramatic Irony
The family will see this sequence and think: She was being generous to the man who was literally destroying her home.

But the reader (if we show the timeline) will see something different:

Maud was trying to buy peace. She was trying to use the marble bequest as a treaty to keep the road away.
The Mayor was ignoring the treaty because he thought the marbles were a reward for his hard work, not a bribe to stop it.
This is a Split Loyalty disaster. Maud tried to bind him with Affinity. He served Creed. Neither declared.

