It makes the Mayor’s guilt internal and unexpressed. He doesn’t have to be a villain. He can be a man who is so focused on his "greater good" that he fails to see the personal cost until it’s too late. And when he finds out Maud died before he could talk to her again, he’s stuck with the marbles and the guilt.

It also makes Maud’s wisdom flawed. She thought she could manage the conflict with paper and money. She couldn’t. She needed to have the hard conversation after changing the will, but she didn’t. She assumed the will was enough.

