Why This Is So Much Stronger
No Villains: The Mayor isn’t greedy or corrupt. He’s competent and civic-minded. He thinks he’s doing right by everyone, including Maud’s legacy.
Maud’s Tragedy: She tried to use money (marbles) to solve a structural problem (infrastructure vs. privacy). It failed because some conflicts can’t be bought off — they have to be negotiated.
The Family’s Rage is Misplaced: They hate him for "destroying" their estate. But if you look at the map, the road is the only viable route to the park entrance. If they block it, they kill the park. If they allow it, they lose their privacy. They are trapped in the same dilemma Maud was.
The Marbles Become a Test: The 100 marbles aren’t just a gift. They’re a vote. By accepting them, the Mayor accepts the responsibility to make the park work without destroying the family. If he fails, he’s not just a bad friend; he’s a man who took the bride price and then slept with the sister.
